more transparency means less conductivity, since the free electrons that give it metallic properties also absorb/scatter light. **So in summary:** - Most people might call ITO a “transparent metal” because it *looks and functions* that way in everyday devices. - Scientists don’t, because its internal physics is that of a doped semiconductor, not a true metal. - A true bulk metal *cannot* be transparent because the very property that makes it metallic (free electrons) blocks visible light. It’s a good example of where everyday language and technical precision diverge—and why we need to be careful when using terms like “transparent metal.”
